window.COURSE = {
units:[
{n:1,title:"Multimedia System",desc:"Foundations, media elements, system structure and data-stream characteristics.",sections:[
["1.1 Introduction to multimedia","Multimedia is the computer-controlled integration of two or more media forms in one application or presentation. Common forms are text, graphics, still images, audio, video and animation. A multimedia system captures or creates media, represents it digitally, stores and processes it, coordinates its timing, and presents it to users. Examples include an interactive learning module, a video-conferencing system, a digital museum exhibit and a multimedia website. The important idea is not simply placing different files together: the media must work as a coordinated experience."],
["1.2 Characteristics and aspects of multimedia","Important characteristics include digital representation, integration of different media, interactivity, non-linear navigation in many applications, and synchronization where media have timing relationships. Integration means combining media in one environment. Interactivity means a user can influence content or its sequence. Synchronization ensures related media are presented at the intended time. Not every multimedia application has the same degree of interactivity: a recorded video is multimedia but may be mostly linear, while an interactive simulation gives the user more control."],
["1.3 Elements of multimedia","Text presents titles, labels, instructions and detailed explanations. Graphics are designed visual elements such as diagrams, icons and charts. Still images represent scenes or objects as pictures. Audio includes speech, music and sound effects. Video captures real-world or rendered motion as a sequence of frames, often with audio. Animation creates apparent motion by changing an object's position, shape or properties over time. A learning application might combine explanatory text, a labelled diagram, a narration track, a demonstration video and an animated process model."],
["1.4 Multimedia system structure","A general multimedia system can be understood as a pipeline: (1) input and capture through a microphone, camera, scanner, keyboard or drawing tool; (2) digitization and encoding to convert or compress media into usable representations; (3) storage in memory, local disks, optical media or network storage; (4) processing and authoring to edit, compose, decode and synchronize media; (5) communication to transfer media when the application is networked; and (6) presentation through a display, speaker, projector or other output device. The application software coordinates the stages. A practical system may repeat stages—for example, stream data is received, buffered, decoded and presented continuously."],
["1.5 Media, medium and media properties","A medium is a form or channel used to represent or deliver information. Media properties include data volume, spatial resolution, colour or amplitude precision, temporal duration, sampling or frame rate, quality, interactivity and timing constraints. Text is usually compact and discrete. A still image has spatial dimensions and colour depth but no inherent playback duration. Audio is a time-dependent sequence of samples. Video combines spatial image information with a sequence of frames and often an audio track. These properties influence storage, processing power, bandwidth and presentation equipment."],
["1.6 Discrete and continuous media","Discrete (time-independent) media are meaningful as individual objects and do not inherently require presentation at a particular rate; examples include text and still images. Continuous (time-dependent) media are consumed over time and need an appropriate temporal rate; examples include audio and video. A still image can be displayed for a chosen duration, but its information is not itself a timed stream. A video file, by contrast, contains frames whose order and intended presentation timing matter. The distinction is important when designing storage, transmission, buffering and synchronization."],
["1.7 Traditional and continuous-media data streams","Traditional data transfer commonly prioritizes correctness, completeness and ordering. A delay may be inconvenient, but a correctly delivered file can often be used later. Continuous-media delivery must also consider when each unit arrives. Audio samples and video frames that arrive too late may be useless for a live presentation even if they are eventually delivered correctly. Important stream characteristics are bit rate (bits per second), sample or frame rate, end-to-end delay, jitter (variation in arrival time), packet loss, ordering, buffering needs and synchronization between streams. A file download and a live video call therefore have different service priorities."],
["1.8 Worked examples: media data size and stream rate","Example 1 — uncompressed image size: a 1920 × 1080 image with 24 bits per pixel requires 1920 × 1080 × 24 = 49,766,400 bits. Dividing by 8 gives 6,220,800 bytes, approximately 5.93 MiB before headers or file-format overhead. Example 2 — PCM audio bitrate: 44,100 samples/second × 16 bits/sample × 2 channels = 1,411,200 bits/second, or 1,411.2 kb/s. These calculations show why multimedia systems use compression and why bandwidth and storage must be planned."],
["1.9 Synchronization and quality considerations","When multiple continuous media are combined, their timing relationship must be preserved. In a video lecture, spoken words should match the speaker's lip movement and slide changes should occur at the intended moment. Buffering helps absorb variation in arrival times, but excessive buffering increases delay. Quality of service is commonly discussed using throughput, delay, jitter and loss. The acceptable values depend on the application: a live conversation is delay-sensitive, while a stored high-quality video download can tolerate more initial waiting."]
],key:["Multimedia is coordinated computer-based integration, not merely a collection of unrelated media files.","Text and still images are typical discrete media; audio and video are typical continuous media.","A multimedia pipeline includes capture, digitization/encoding, storage, processing, communication and presentation.","Continuous-media systems must manage timing, rate, delay, jitter, loss and synchronization.","Raw media can require substantial storage and bandwidth, making compression important."],questions:["Define a multimedia system. Explain its characteristics and major elements with examples.","Draw and explain the general architecture or processing pipeline of a multimedia system.","Differentiate discrete and continuous media. Give at least three examples of each.","Compare traditional data transfer with continuous-media stream delivery.","Define bit rate, frame rate, delay and jitter. Explain their importance in multimedia systems.","A 1280 × 720 image uses 24 bits per pixel. Calculate its uncompressed size in bits and bytes.","Calculate the bitrate of 48 kHz, 16-bit, stereo PCM audio.","Explain why synchronization and buffering are required in a multimedia presentation."]},
{n:2,title:"Sound and Audio",desc:"Sound representation, digital audio formats, MIDI and speech processing.",sections:[
["2.1 Fundamentals of sound","Sound is a mechanical disturbance that travels through an elastic medium as a wave. A vibrating source creates pressure variations that the ear can detect. Frequency, measured in hertz (Hz), is the number of cycles per second and is perceived mainly as pitch. Amplitude describes signal magnitude and is related to loudness, although perceived loudness also depends on frequency and listening conditions. Waveform shape and harmonic content contribute to timbre, which helps distinguish instruments playing the same note. The approximate human hearing range is often stated as 20 Hz to 20 kHz, but it varies with age and individual hearing."],
["2.2 Analogue and digital audio","An analogue audio signal varies continuously with time and amplitude. A computer stores digital audio as numerical samples. The analogue-to-digital conversion process involves sampling, quantization and binary encoding. Sampling measures the waveform at regular time intervals; quantization maps each measured amplitude to one of a finite number of levels; encoding stores each quantized value as bits. Digital-to-analogue conversion reconstructs a continuous output signal from the stored samples for playback."],
["2.3 Sampling theorem and sampling rate","The sampling rate is the number of samples captured per second, measured in samples/s or Hz. The Nyquist-Shannon sampling principle states that a band-limited signal must be sampled at a rate greater than twice its highest frequency component for ideal reconstruction. Thus, a signal containing frequencies up to 20 kHz requires a sampling rate above 40 kHz in theory; practical systems use a margin and anti-aliasing filters. If the sampling rate is too low, aliasing causes high-frequency components to appear as incorrect lower frequencies. Common audio rates include 44.1 kHz and 48 kHz."],
["2.4 Quantization and bit depth","Bit depth is the number of bits used to represent each sample. With n bits, there are 2^n possible quantization codes. For example, 8-bit audio has 256 levels, while 16-bit audio has 65,536 levels. More levels generally reduce quantization error and increase the potential dynamic range, but also increase data size. Quantization noise is the difference between the original sample value and its quantized representation. In a simplified uniform quantizer, each sample is mapped to the nearest allowed level."],
["2.5 PCM representation and audio calculations","Pulse Code Modulation (PCM) represents audio by sampling, quantizing and encoding sample values. For uncompressed PCM, bitrate = sampling rate × bits per sample × number of channels. Example: 48,000 samples/s × 16 bits × 2 channels = 1,536,000 bits/s = 1,536 kb/s. To estimate uncompressed storage: size in bits = bitrate × duration in seconds. A 60-second recording at this rate requires 1,536,000 × 60 = 92,160,000 bits = 11,520,000 bytes, approximately 10.99 MiB, before file headers and metadata. Always distinguish bits (b) from bytes (B); 8 bits = 1 byte."],
["2.6 Audio quality and file formats","Audio quality depends on sampling rate, bit depth, channel count, microphone and recording conditions, processing and codec settings. WAV and AIFF are container formats often used for uncompressed PCM. FLAC compresses audio losslessly, preserving the original decoded samples. MP3 and AAC use lossy perceptual coding to reduce file size by removing or representing less audible information. A container is not necessarily a codec: a WAV file commonly contains PCM, but the extension alone does not guarantee every property of the encoded stream."],
["2.7 MIDI: purpose and representation","MIDI means Musical Instrument Digital Interface. It is a communication standard for musical performance and control information; it does not normally store a recording of the actual sound wave. A MIDI sequence can specify which note to play, when to start and stop it, how strongly it is played and which instrument or sound program to use. The receiving synthesizer or software instrument generates the audio. Consequently, the same MIDI sequence may sound different on different synthesizers."],
["2.8 MIDI messages and devices","Common MIDI messages include Note On, Note Off, velocity, Program Change, Control Change, Pitch Bend and timing/clock messages. A MIDI channel allows messages to address a particular part or instrument. A keyboard or pad controller sends performance messages; a synthesizer or sound module turns them into sound; a sequencer records, edits and plays event sequences; and a computer with MIDI software can coordinate virtual instruments. A MIDI file stores event data and timing, so its playback depends on the available sound source."],
["2.9 Speech generation","Speech generation produces spoken output from linguistic input. Text-to-speech (TTS) systems typically normalize text, analyze linguistic structure, generate a pronunciation/phonetic representation and synthesize a speech waveform. Speech can also be generated or recorded by a human speaker using a microphone and audio interface. Output quality is influenced by pronunciation, prosody, speaking rate, voice characteristics and synthesis method."],
["2.10 Speech analysis: recognition and understanding","Speech analysis begins with an audio signal and may include preprocessing, segmentation and feature extraction. Automatic speech recognition (ASR) estimates the words spoken. Speech understanding goes further by interpreting meaning, intent, entities or commands in context. For example, recognizing the words “set an alarm for six” is different from interpreting the request and creating the alarm. Noise, accent, speaking rate, microphone quality and background voices can reduce accuracy."],
["2.11 Speech transmission","Speech transmission converts speech to a digital representation, may compress it with a speech codec, packetizes it for a network and reconstructs it at the receiver. A speech codec aims to reduce bandwidth while maintaining intelligibility. Real-time voice communication is sensitive to end-to-end delay, jitter and packet loss. Jitter buffers smooth variable packet arrival times, but larger buffers can add conversational delay. Echo cancellation and noise suppression may improve call quality."]
],key:["Digital audio conversion involves sampling, quantization and binary encoding.","The Nyquist principle requires a sampling rate greater than twice the highest frequency in a band-limited signal; anti-aliasing filters are used in practice.","For n-bit quantization, the number of possible levels is 2^n.","PCM bitrate = sampling rate × bit depth × channel count; storage also depends on duration.","FLAC is lossless; MP3 and AAC are lossy perceptual formats. WAV and AIFF are containers commonly used for PCM.","MIDI stores performance/control events; a synthesizer or software instrument generates the sound.","Speech recognition identifies spoken words; speech understanding interprets their meaning and intent."],questions:["Explain the complete analogue-to-digital audio conversion process with a labelled diagram.","State the Nyquist sampling principle. What is aliasing and how can it be reduced?","Explain quantization, bit depth and quantization noise.","A 44.1 kHz, 16-bit, stereo PCM recording lasts 3 minutes. Calculate its bitrate and approximate uncompressed size in MB and MiB.","Differentiate WAV, AIFF, FLAC, MP3 and AAC.","What is MIDI? Explain its messages, devices and working with a simple block diagram.","Differentiate speech generation, speech recognition and speech understanding.","Explain the stages and challenges of speech transmission."]},
{n:3,title:"Image and Graphics",desc:"Raster and vector representation, image formats, processing, synthesis and analysis.",sections:[
["3.1 Digital image fundamentals","A digital image is a two-dimensional representation of visual information. In a raster image, it is organized as a grid of picture elements called pixels. Each pixel stores one or more numerical values describing brightness or colour. A grayscale image may store one intensity value per pixel; a colour image commonly stores multiple colour components. Image quality depends on acquisition conditions, spatial resolution, bit depth, compression and display characteristics."],
["3.2 Resolution, pixel dimensions and aspect ratio","Image dimensions state the number of pixels horizontally and vertically, for example 1920 × 1080. The total pixel count is width × height. Resolution is sometimes used to mean pixel dimensions and sometimes pixel density (such as pixels per inch); state which meaning is intended. Aspect ratio is width divided by height. A 1920 × 1080 image has an aspect ratio of 16:9. Increasing pixel dimensions can preserve finer spatial detail, but increases storage and processing requirements."],
["3.3 Colour depth and colour models","Colour depth is the number of bits used to represent a pixel or its components. With b bits per pixel, the maximum number of possible pixel values is 2^b. An 8-bit grayscale image can represent 256 intensity levels. A typical 24-bit RGB image uses 8 bits for red, 8 for green and 8 for blue, allowing 256^3 = 16,777,216 combinations. RGB is an additive colour model used by displays. CMYK is a subtractive model commonly used in colour printing. Grayscale represents shades of intensity; indexed colour stores pixel references to a limited colour palette."],
["3.4 Raster and vector graphics","Raster graphics represent an image as a fixed grid of pixels. They are suitable for photographs and detailed textures, but enlarging them beyond their original dimensions can reveal pixelation. Vector graphics represent objects through mathematical descriptions such as points, lines, curves, paths, fills and strokes. They are well suited to logos, diagrams and illustrations because they can be scaled without becoming pixelated in the same way. Vector artwork may be rasterized when displayed or exported to a pixel-based format."],
["3.5 Image file formats","BMP is a raster format often associated with relatively simple pixel storage and potentially large files. TIFF is a flexible image container used in workflows that may require high quality and metadata; compression options vary. JPEG uses lossy compression and is widely used for photographs. PNG uses lossless compression and supports transparency, making it useful for graphics and interface assets. GIF uses a limited indexed colour palette and supports simple frame-based animation. SVG is a vector graphics format based on shapes and paths. Format choice depends on image content, transparency, editing needs, compatibility and file-size constraints."],
["3.6 Image acquisition and digitization","Images may be acquired using digital cameras, scanners, video frames or generated graphics. An acquisition system converts optical information into sensor measurements and digital values. Important factors include sensor quality, focus, lighting, exposure, sampling resolution, colour response and noise. Scanning or digitizing an analogue image requires spatial sampling and quantization of intensity or colour. Poor acquisition cannot always be repaired by later processing."],
["3.7 Image-processing pipeline","A common image-processing workflow is: acquire the image; inspect dimensions and colour properties; preprocess to correct noise or illumination; enhance relevant details; transform or segment regions; extract features; interpret or export the result. Not every task needs every stage. Enhancement aims to make an image more useful or visually clear, while analysis aims to obtain information from it. Preserve an original copy so that edits can be reversed or compared."],
["3.8 Common image-processing operations","Cropping selects a region of interest. Resizing changes pixel dimensions and requires interpolation when creating new pixel values. Rotation and geometric transforms change spatial arrangement. Brightness changes overall intensity; contrast changes the separation between dark and light values. Histogram equalization redistributes intensity values to improve contrast in some images. Smoothing filters reduce noise but may blur edges; sharpening emphasizes local detail but can amplify noise. Thresholding maps pixels to classes, often producing a binary image. Edge detection highlights rapid intensity changes."],
["3.9 Image synthesis and image analysis","Image synthesis creates visual output from data, models, shapes or algorithms—for example, rendering a 3D scene or drawing a chart. Image analysis extracts useful information from an image, such as edges, regions, object locations, colour statistics or measurements. A simple analysis system may perform acquisition, preprocessing, segmentation, feature extraction and classification or interpretation. Synthesis answers “how can an image be generated?” while analysis asks “what information can be obtained from this image?”"],
["3.10 Image transmission and compression","Images sent over a network are often compressed to reduce storage and transmission time. The sender encodes the image, divides data into transport units if needed and transmits it through a channel; the receiver reconstructs and decodes it. Lossless compression preserves all original image information, while lossy compression reduces size by discarding or approximating information. Important trade-offs include file size, visual quality, bandwidth, delay, packet loss and the need for progressive or robust delivery."],
["3.11 Worked calculations: image storage","Example 1 — 800 × 600, 8-bit grayscale: pixels = 800 × 600 = 480,000. Raw size = 480,000 × 8 = 3,840,000 bits = 480,000 bytes (about 468.75 KiB), ignoring headers and row padding. Example 2 — 1920 × 1080, 24-bit RGB: 1920 × 1080 × 24 = 49,766,400 bits; divide by 8 = 6,220,800 bytes (about 5.93 MiB). Example 3 — a 1024 × 1024 image at 32 bits per pixel: 1,048,576 × 32 = 33,554,432 bits = 4,194,304 bytes (4 MiB). These are raw sizes; actual compressed file sizes vary with content and format settings."]
],key:["A raster image is a pixel grid; vector graphics are described using mathematical shapes and paths.","Pixel dimensions, pixel density and aspect ratio describe different image properties.","For b bits per pixel, the theoretical number of possible pixel values is 2^b.","RGB is additive and common for displays; CMYK is subtractive and common in printing.","JPEG is lossy; PNG is lossless and supports transparency; SVG is vector-based.","Image enhancement improves appearance or usefulness; image analysis extracts information.","Raw image size in bytes = width × height × bits per pixel ÷ 8, before overhead."],questions:["Explain digital image representation, resolution, aspect ratio and colour depth.","Compare raster and vector graphics with suitable applications.","Explain RGB, CMYK, grayscale and indexed colour models.","Compare BMP, TIFF, JPEG, PNG, GIF and SVG formats.","Describe the stages of a general image-processing pipeline.","Explain cropping, resizing, histogram equalization, smoothing, sharpening and thresholding.","Differentiate image synthesis and image analysis with examples.","A 1280 × 720 image uses 24 bits per pixel. Calculate its raw size in bits, bytes and MiB.","A 640 × 480 grayscale image uses 8 bits per pixel. Calculate its uncompressed storage requirement.","Discuss the main trade-offs involved in image transmission."]},
{n:4,title:"Video and Animation",desc:"Video signal representation, formats, transmission and computer animation.",sections:[
["4.1 Introduction to video","Video is a visual medium that represents changing scenes over time. Digital video is commonly stored as a sequence of still images called frames, displayed in temporal order at a specified frame rate. Many video programs also contain audio, subtitles, captions and metadata. Video is a continuous medium: both the spatial information in each frame and the timing between frames contribute to the presentation."],
["4.2 Analogue and digital video signals","Analogue video represents picture information as continuously varying electrical signals. Traditional analogue systems use scanning and synchronization signals to control how a display reconstructs each picture. Digital video represents picture samples as numerical values arranged into frames. Digital representation supports copying, editing, compression and network transmission, although conversion from analogue sources requires sampling and quantization. Digital video systems must also represent frame timing and, where applicable, audio timing."],
["4.3 Video frame, resolution and aspect ratio","A frame is one still image in a video sequence. Frame dimensions are expressed as width × height in pixels; examples include 1280 × 720 and 1920 × 1080. Frame rate is the number of frames displayed each second, measured in fps. A higher frame rate can make motion appear smoother but requires more data if other settings remain unchanged. Aspect ratio describes the relationship between frame width and height: 16:9 is common for widescreen video, while 4:3 is a traditional format. Resolution, frame rate and aspect ratio describe different properties."],
["4.4 Video data rate and storage calculation","Uncompressed video data rate can be estimated as width × height × bits per pixel × frames per second. For example, 1280 × 720 video at 24 bits per pixel and 30 fps requires 1280 × 720 × 24 × 30 = 663,552,000 bits/s, or about 663.6 Mb/s, before audio and overhead. For a 10-second clip, raw data would be about 6.64 billion bits. Real video is usually compressed because raw video requires very high storage and bandwidth. Actual compressed bitrate depends on codec, quality settings, motion, frame rate, resolution and content complexity."],
["4.5 Colour representation and scanning concepts","Digital video frames use colour components to represent picture information. RGB represents red, green and blue components. Video systems commonly use YCbCr, separating luma (brightness-related information) from chroma (colour-difference information). Chroma subsampling, such as 4:2:0, reduces colour detail relative to luma to save data while often retaining acceptable perceived quality. Interlaced scanning divides a frame into two fields displayed at different times; progressive scanning displays complete frames in sequence. These approaches affect motion portrayal, display compatibility and processing."],
["4.6 Video formats, containers and codecs","A container is a file structure that packages one or more media streams and associated metadata. MP4, MOV and MKV are examples of containers. A codec is an encoding/decoding method used to compress and reconstruct media; examples include H.264/AVC, H.265/HEVC and AV1. A container does not itself specify one unique codec. The same container can hold streams encoded with different codecs, subject to compatibility. When selecting a format, consider device support, quality, bitrate, editing needs and intended delivery."],
["4.7 Video compression","Video compression reduces the data needed to store or transmit moving pictures. Many codecs exploit spatial redundancy within a frame and temporal redundancy between frames. Intra-frame coding compresses information within an individual frame. Inter-frame coding predicts frames from other frames and encodes differences or motion information. Compression may be lossy or lossless, although consumer delivery commonly uses lossy compression. Higher compression can reduce bitrate but may introduce blocking, blurring, ringing or motion artifacts."],
["4.8 Video transmission and streaming","Video can be downloaded as a complete file or streamed so playback begins before the entire program arrives. Streaming clients commonly use buffers to absorb variation in network delivery. Adaptive bitrate streaming offers multiple encoded versions and selects a suitable quality according to available throughput and device conditions. Important network factors include bandwidth/throughput, end-to-end delay, jitter, packet loss and congestion. Live interactive video is especially sensitive to delay; on-demand playback can often use a larger buffer to reduce interruptions."],
["4.9 Animation fundamentals","Animation creates the illusion of movement by displaying a sequence of images or changing object states over time. Traditional frame-by-frame animation draws each frame. Computer animation can manipulate object position, rotation, scale, shape, colour, camera and lighting. A storyboard plans the sequence; a timeline organizes events; key poses define important moments; rendering generates the final frames."],
["4.10 Keyframes, tweening and interpolation","A keyframe specifies an important state of an animated object at a particular time, such as its position or rotation. Tweening (in-betweening) generates intermediate states between keyframes. Linear interpolation changes a property at a constant rate; easing functions accelerate or decelerate motion to make it feel more natural. The animator controls timing (when an action occurs) and spacing (how far an object moves between frames). Poor timing can make an action feel too slow, too fast or weightless."],
["4.11 Principles of animation","Useful animation principles include anticipation (preparing the viewer for an action), squash and stretch (communicating flexibility and impact while preserving apparent volume), staging (making the action clear), follow-through and overlapping action (allowing parts to continue moving at different times), arcs (natural curved paths), timing and spacing, and exaggeration where appropriate. These principles help make motion readable and expressive; not every style requires all principles equally."],
["4.12 Animation control, languages and delivery","Animation can be authored with timeline-based applications, scripting languages, graphics libraries and 2D/3D tools. A program may update object properties over time, respond to user input and render frames. In 3D animation, the workflow may include modelling, rigging, materials, lighting, camera setup, animation, rendering and compositing. Animation can be delivered as a rendered video or as interactive scene data evaluated on the receiving device."]
],key:["Digital video is a time-ordered sequence of frames; frame rate and frame dimensions are separate properties.","Uncompressed video rate depends on width, height, bits per pixel and frames per second.","A container packages streams and metadata; a codec compresses and reconstructs media.","Video compression exploits spatial and temporal redundancy.","Streaming uses buffering; adaptive bitrate delivery can respond to changing network conditions.","Keyframes define important states; tweening/interpolation creates intermediate states.","Timing, spacing, anticipation, staging and follow-through improve animated motion."],questions:["Explain analogue and digital video representation.","Define frame, resolution, frame rate and aspect ratio. Explain their effects on video quality.","A 640 × 480, 24-bit video runs at 25 fps. Calculate its uncompressed data rate in bits/s and Mb/s.","Differentiate a video container and a codec with examples.","Explain intra-frame and inter-frame video compression.","Discuss the major challenges in video streaming and transmission.","Explain keyframes, tweening and linear interpolation.","Describe important principles of computer animation with examples.","Explain a typical 3D animation production workflow."]},
{n:5,title:"Data Compression",desc:"Coding requirements, source and entropy coding, RLE, Huffman, predictive and standards.",sections:[
["5.1 Basic concepts and requirements","Compression reduces the number of bits required to represent information. Lossless compression reconstructs the original exactly; lossy compression discards information to achieve higher reduction. A coding method is judged by compression ratio, quality/distortion, speed, complexity, memory and error sensitivity."],
["5.2 Source, entropy and hybrid coding","Source coding represents the source efficiently by exploiting redundancy or perceptual properties. Entropy coding assigns shorter codes to more probable symbols. Hybrid systems combine techniques—for example, prediction/transform, quantization and entropy coding."],
["5.3 Run-Length Encoding (RLE)","RLE replaces consecutive repeated symbols with a symbol and its run length. Example: AAAAABBCC becomes (A,5)(B,2)(C,2). It is effective for long runs but may be inefficient when data has few repetitions."],
["5.4 Huffman coding","Huffman coding builds a binary tree by repeatedly combining the two least-probable symbols. The resulting prefix codes are shorter for frequent symbols and longer for rare symbols. Because no codeword is a prefix of another, decoding is unambiguous. Work through a frequency table, tree construction and average code length when practising."],
["5.5 Predictive and adaptive compression","Predictive coding encodes the difference between a value and a prediction, often reducing redundancy in correlated data. Adaptive methods update coding statistics or models as data is processed, allowing the code to respond to changing symbol distributions."],
["5.6 JPEG, H.261, MPEG and DVI","JPEG is a still-image coding family commonly associated with block transforms, quantization and entropy coding. H.261 (also called Px64) was designed for video telephony/conferencing at multiples of 64 kb/s. MPEG refers to a family of standards for coded audio/video. DVI (Digital Video Interactive) is a multimedia technology associated with digital video/audio compression and playback. Exact profiles and versions matter when comparing standards."]
],key:["Compression ratio = original size ÷ compressed size (state the convention used).","Lossless coding permits exact reconstruction; lossy coding trades fidelity for size.","RLE, Huffman and predictive coding exploit different kinds of redundancy."],questions:["Differentiate lossless and lossy compression.","Encode a sample string using RLE.","Explain Huffman coding with a worked example.","Explain predictive coding and adaptive compression.","Write notes on JPEG, H.261, MPEG and DVI."]},
{n:6,title:"Optical Storage Media",desc:"Optical storage principles, video discs, Blu-ray, CD formats and recording.",sections:[
["6.1 Basic optical storage technology","Optical discs store information as physical or optical changes read by a focused laser. A disc drive focuses a laser on a track and detects reflected-light differences. Capacity and performance depend on track pitch, laser wavelength, recording layer, data density and error correction."],
["6.2 Video disc fundamentals","Video discs are optical media designed to store and reproduce video and associated information. Their operation combines physical disc layout, modulation, error correction, addressing and playback electronics. Distinguish the disc medium from the video compression and file/container formats used."],
["6.3 Blu-ray Disc","Blu-ray uses a blue-violet laser with a shorter wavelength than the red laser used by DVD, enabling smaller focused spots and higher data density. Blu-ray formats include read-only, recordable and rewritable variants. Actual capacity depends on disc layer configuration and specification."],
["6.4 CD audio, CD-ROM and extended architecture","Audio CD stores digital audio using a standardized disc structure and playback timing. CD-ROM stores computer data and uses error detection/correction suitable for data retrieval. Extended CD architectures can combine audio and data sessions/tracks; compatibility depends on the disc layout and player."],
["6.5 Write-once and magneto-optical principles","Write-once optical media allow data to be recorded but not normally erased and rewritten. Magneto-optical storage uses laser heating together with a magnetic field to change the recording state; reading is optical. These approaches differ in rewritability, recording mechanism and media handling."]
],key:["Optical storage uses laser light and reflected-light detection.","Blu-ray's shorter-wavelength laser supports higher data density than DVD.","CD audio and CD-ROM have different data and playback requirements."],questions:["Explain the basic technology of optical storage.","Why can Blu-ray store more data than DVD?","Differentiate CD audio and CD-ROM.","Explain write-once and magneto-optical recording."]},
{n:7,title:"Multimedia Operating System",desc:"Real-time systems, resource management, scheduling algorithms and architecture.",sections:[
["7.1 Introduction and real-time systems","A multimedia operating system manages processors, memory, storage, devices and communication so media can be captured and presented reliably. A real-time system must produce results within timing constraints. Correctness can depend on both the result and when it is delivered."],
["7.2 Resource-management requirements","Continuous media require predictable CPU time, memory buffers, storage bandwidth, network bandwidth and device access. Resource management should control contention, reserve or allocate capacity, maintain quality of service and avoid buffer underflow/overflow."],
["7.3 Allocation schemes and continuous-media resource model","Resources may be allocated statically in advance or dynamically as demand changes. A continuous-media model considers periodic data units, presentation deadlines, processing demand, buffer capacity and delivery rate. Admission control can reject a new stream if accepting it would violate existing guarantees."],
["7.4 Process management and real-time scheduling","Real-time processes may be periodic (recurring jobs) or aperiodic. A task can be described by execution time C, period T and deadline D. Scheduling decides which ready task executes next while meeting deadlines."],
["7.5 Earliest Deadline First (EDF)","EDF is a dynamic-priority algorithm: the ready task with the earliest absolute deadline is selected. Priorities can change as deadlines change. For independent preemptible periodic tasks on a single processor with deadlines equal to periods, a common feasibility test is total utilization U = Σ(Ci/Ti) ≤ 1 under the ideal assumptions."],
["7.6 Rate Monotonic (RM)","RM is a fixed-priority algorithm: tasks with shorter periods receive higher priority. Under classic assumptions (independent periodic tasks, preemptive fixed-priority scheduling, deadlines equal to periods), a sufficient utilization bound for n tasks is U ≤ n(2^(1/n) − 1). This bound is sufficient, not necessary; some task sets above it may still be schedulable."],
["7.7 System architecture","A multimedia OS architecture coordinates applications, multimedia middleware/services, scheduling and resource management, device drivers and hardware. Timing-aware services connect application quality requirements to resource allocation."]
],key:["Real-time correctness includes meeting deadlines.","EDF assigns dynamic priority by earliest deadline; RM uses fixed priority by shortest period.","For RM, the utilization bound is sufficient under its assumptions, not a universal exact test."],questions:["Explain resource-management requirements of a multimedia OS.","Compare EDF and Rate Monotonic scheduling.","For tasks (C,T) = (1,4), (2,8), calculate utilization.","Explain why multimedia applications need real-time processing."]},
{n:8,title:"Multimedia Communication System",desc:"Application and transport subsystems, QoS and resource management.",sections:[
["8.1 Introduction","A multimedia communication system transfers integrated media between users or systems. It must support media with different timing, bandwidth, reliability and interaction requirements."],
["8.2 Application subsystem","The application subsystem provides user-facing functions such as conferencing, streaming, collaboration or media sharing. It handles media capture/playback, session control, encoding choices, synchronization and application-specific quality requirements."],
["8.3 Transport subsystem","The transport subsystem moves data across the network. It may use reliable or best-effort transport depending on the application. It must consider packetization, congestion, delay, jitter, loss, ordering and bandwidth. Real-time interactive media often values timely delivery over retransmitting data that arrives too late."],
["8.4 Quality of Service (QoS)","QoS describes measurable service characteristics such as throughput, end-to-end delay, jitter, packet loss and availability. Different applications have different acceptable ranges: a file download prioritizes integrity, while a live conversation is highly sensitive to delay."],
["8.5 Resource management","Resource management allocates bandwidth, buffers, processing and network paths to meet application needs. Techniques include admission control, traffic shaping, scheduling, reservation, prioritization and adaptive bitrate/quality. A system must balance competing flows fairly while protecting time-sensitive media."]
],key:["Application subsystem provides media service; transport subsystem delivers data.","QoS commonly includes throughput, delay, jitter and loss.","Interactive voice/video is particularly sensitive to latency and jitter."],questions:["Draw and explain a multimedia communication system.","Explain the functions of application and transport subsystems.","Define QoS and discuss its parameters.","Describe resource-management techniques for multimedia communication."]},
{n:9,title:"Documentation, Hypertext and MHEG",desc:"Multimedia documents, architecture, hypermedia, SGML, ODA and MHEG.",sections:[
["9.1 Documents and multimedia data","A multimedia document can combine text, images, audio, video, graphics and references to external resources. Its structure describes content, relationships, presentation and metadata. Manipulation includes creation, editing, storage, retrieval, linking and presentation."],
["9.2 Architecture and manipulation","A document architecture defines logical structure and how content is represented or exchanged. Multimedia document systems may separate content, structure, style and presentation. Editing operations can change nodes, media objects, links, timing and layout."],
["9.3 Hypertext and hypermedia","Hypertext links textual information; hypermedia extends linking to multimedia objects. A node is a unit of information and a link expresses a relationship or navigation path between nodes. Links may be navigational or semantic and can connect content within or across documents."],
["9.4 Hypermedia system architecture","A hypermedia system typically includes nodes, links, anchors, a navigation mechanism, a presentation engine and a storage/retrieval layer. The user follows links to explore a non-linear information space. Good design makes link meaning and navigation state clear."],
["9.5 SGML, ODA and MHEG","SGML (Standard Generalized Markup Language) is a meta-language for defining markup languages and document structures. ODA (Office Document Architecture) specifies a structured approach to representing and exchanging office documents, including logical and layout structure. MHEG (Multimedia and Hypermedia information coding Expert Group) refers to standards for coded representation and exchange of multimedia and hypermedia information."]
],key:["Hypertext links text; hypermedia links multiple media forms.","Nodes hold information; links connect nodes through anchors or references.","SGML, ODA and MHEG address different document or multimedia representation needs."],questions:["Define hypertext and hypermedia. Explain nodes and links.","Draw a basic hypermedia system architecture.","Write short notes on SGML, ODA and MHEG.","Explain multimedia document structure and manipulation."]},
{n:10,title:"Synchronization",desc:"Temporal notation, presentation requirements, reference models and specifications.",sections:[
["10.1 Introduction","Synchronization coordinates media objects in time. In a multimedia presentation, audio, video, subtitles, animation and user events must be presented in the intended temporal relationship."],
["10.2 Notation of synchronization","Synchronization can be described using temporal relationships such as before, after, during, starts, finishes, meets and overlaps. A timeline or event graph can express when media objects begin, end and relate to each other."],
["10.3 Presentation requirements","A presentation may require intra-media synchronization (correct timing within one stream, such as video frame pacing) and inter-media synchronization (relationships between streams, such as lip synchronization between audio and video). Buffering, clock drift, delay and jitter can affect presentation."],
["10.4 Reference model for multimedia synchronization","A conceptual model includes media sources, clocks/timestamps, synchronization control, buffers, a scheduler and presentation devices. Timestamps and timing information allow the system to coordinate streams despite variable delivery times."],
["10.5 Synchronization specification","A synchronization specification describes temporal constraints, object start/end times, duration, ordering, parallelism and event-triggered behavior. It should identify timing references and acceptable tolerances. The supplied syllabus lists a section 10.6 but its title is not legible in the provided outline; confirm that item with the official course document."]
],key:["Intra-media synchronization concerns timing within one stream; inter-media concerns relationships between streams.","Timestamps, clocks, buffers and schedulers help coordinate presentation.","Lip-sync is a common inter-media synchronization requirement."],questions:["Define multimedia synchronization and explain its need.","Differentiate intra-media and inter-media synchronization.","Explain a reference model for multimedia synchronization.","Describe temporal relationships used in synchronization notation."]},
{n:11,title:"Abstraction of Programming",desc:"Abstraction levels, libraries, system software, toolkits, languages and OOP.",sections:[
["11.1 Introduction","Programming abstraction hides lower-level implementation details and exposes concepts suitable for the task. Multimedia development can range from direct hardware control to high-level authoring tools."],
["11.2 Abstraction levels","At a low level, developers manage devices, buffers and data formats. System-level APIs expose operating-system services. Libraries and frameworks provide reusable media operations. Toolkits offer higher-level components and workflows. High-level languages and visual authoring environments express application behavior with less implementation detail."],
["11.3 Libraries and system software","Libraries provide reusable functions for tasks such as image manipulation, audio processing, video decoding or graphics rendering. System software includes operating systems, drivers, codecs and runtime services that allow applications to use hardware and media resources."],
["11.4 Toolkits and higher programming languages","A toolkit combines APIs, components, utilities and sometimes visual editors to accelerate development. Higher-level languages improve readability and productivity by abstracting memory, device and platform details, though performance-critical sections may still require lower-level optimization."],
["11.5 Object-oriented approaches","Object-oriented design models application entities as objects with state and behavior. Encapsulation groups data and operations; abstraction exposes essential behavior; inheritance supports specialization; polymorphism allows a common interface to work with different implementations. Multimedia objects might represent a media clip, player, timeline, track or effect."]
],key:["Higher abstraction reduces low-level detail but may reduce direct control.","Libraries and toolkits promote reuse.","OOP concepts include encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance and polymorphism."],questions:["Explain levels of abstraction in multimedia programming.","Differentiate a library, toolkit and system software.","Discuss object-oriented approaches to multimedia application development.","Explain the role of high-level programming languages."]},
{n:12,title:"Multimedia Application",desc:"On-demand media, conferencing, education, industry, archives, entertainment and trends.",sections:[
["12.1 Video-on-demand (VoD)","VoD allows a user to select and view stored video when requested rather than following a fixed broadcast schedule. A typical system includes content storage, media server, network, client application, streaming protocol and playback buffer. Key concerns are scalability, bandwidth, latency, content protection and service quality."],
["12.2 Video conferencing","Video conferencing enables real-time audio/video communication between two or more endpoints. Components include cameras, microphones, codecs, network transport, echo/noise control, display and session management. Delay, jitter and packet loss strongly affect conversational quality."],
["12.3 Educational and industrial applications","Education uses multimedia for interactive lessons, demonstrations, simulations, virtual labs and distance learning. Industry uses it for training, product visualization, maintenance instructions, design review, process monitoring and simulation."],
["12.4 Information systems","Multimedia information systems organize and retrieve mixed media. They may use metadata, indexing, databases, search, access control and content-management functions to help users locate relevant objects."],
["12.5 Multimedia archives and digital libraries","Archives and digital libraries preserve, describe, manage and provide access to digital media collections. Important concerns include metadata, formats, preservation, migration, rights, authenticity, storage redundancy and long-term accessibility."],
["12.6 Media entertainment and editors","Entertainment includes games, films, music, animation and interactive experiences. Media editors support operations such as cutting, sequencing, compositing, colour correction, audio mixing, effects and rendering."],
["12.7 Trends","Current directions include cloud media processing, mobile and immersive experiences, interactive streaming, AI-assisted editing and search, personalized delivery, higher-resolution media and efficient codecs. Evaluate trends by their technical requirements, accessibility, privacy, cost and user value."]
],key:["VoD is user-selected on-demand playback; conferencing is interactive real-time communication.","Digital libraries need metadata, preservation and reliable retrieval.","Multimedia applications span education, industry, information systems and entertainment."],questions:["Explain the architecture of a video-on-demand system.","Discuss the components and challenges of video conferencing.","Explain multimedia applications in education and industry.","Discuss multimedia archives and digital libraries.","Describe emerging trends in multimedia applications."]}
],
labs:[
{title:"Multimedia integration",goal:"Combine multiple media elements into a coherent, synchronized presentation.",steps:["Plan a short presentation and list required text, still images, audio, speech, music, video and animation assets.","Prepare media in suitable formats and document source, dimensions, duration and sample/frame rates.","Import assets into an authoring or editing environment and arrange them on tracks or a timeline.","Set start times, durations, transitions and audio-video synchronization.","Preview, correct timing and quality problems, then export and test the final output."],deliver:"Submit the project file, exported presentation, asset list and a short explanation of integration and synchronization decisions."},
{title:"Image enhancement (Photoshop / equivalent)",goal:"Improve image appearance using non-destructive editing and appropriate export settings.",steps:["Open an original image and preserve an untouched copy.","Inspect dimensions, colour mode and histogram.","Apply crop/straighten, tonal correction, colour balance and suitable noise reduction.","Use layers and masks for local adjustments; sharpen carefully after resizing.","Export an appropriate web or print version and compare it with the original."],deliver:"Submit before/after images, editable project file and a note describing each enhancement."},
{title:"2D / 3D animation",goal:"Create a short animation using keyframes and a suitable animation tool or graphics API.",steps:["Define a simple object, action and storyboard.","Create assets or 3D geometry; set scene, camera and lighting if applicable.","Set key poses at chosen frames and interpolate in-between motion.","Adjust timing, spacing and easing; preview at the target frame rate.","Render/export and inspect continuity, visual quality and file size."],deliver:"Submit storyboard, source/project file, rendered animation and a brief explanation of keyframes."},
{title:"JPEG compression algorithm",goal:"Understand and demonstrate the main stages of JPEG-style still-image compression.",steps:["Convert image data to a suitable colour representation and optionally subsample chroma.","Divide image components into 8×8 blocks and level-shift sample values.","Apply the discrete cosine transform (DCT) to each block.","Quantize transform coefficients; this is the main lossy stage.","Reorder coefficients in a zigzag pattern, encode runs and apply entropy coding.","Decode the result and compare image quality and compression ratio."],deliver:"Submit algorithm flowchart, sample input/output, compression-ratio calculation and a discussion of quantization artifacts."},
{title:"Real-time scheduling algorithm",goal:"Implement and compare EDF and Rate Monotonic scheduling on a small periodic task set.",steps:["Represent each task with execution time C, period T and deadline D.","Calculate utilization U = Σ(C/T) and state the scheduling assumptions.","For EDF, select the ready job with the earliest absolute deadline.","For RM, assign fixed priority to the task with the shortest period.","Simulate releases, execution and deadlines on a timeline; record missed deadlines.","Compare schedule behavior and explain why a utilization bound is not always an exact schedulability test."],deliver:"Submit source code, task table, Gantt/timeline chart, utilization calculations and comparison."}
],
glossary:[
["Animation","A technique that creates perceived motion by displaying a sequence of images or states."],["Bit depth","Number of bits used to represent each sample or pixel component."],["Bitrate","Number of bits processed or transmitted per unit time."],["Codec","A coder/decoder used to encode and decode media data."],["Compression ratio","Original data size divided by compressed data size."],["Continuous media","Time-dependent media such as audio and video."],["DCT","Discrete Cosine Transform; a transform used in image/video coding."],["EDF","Earliest Deadline First; dynamic-priority real-time scheduling."],["Frame rate","Number of video or animation frames displayed per second."],["Hypermedia","A linked information system that can connect text and other media."],["Jitter","Variation in packet or media-unit arrival timing."],["MIDI","A protocol for musical performance and control messages."],["Node","A unit of information in a hypertext or hypermedia system."],["PCM","Pulse Code Modulation; digital representation using sampled and quantized values."],["QoS","Quality of Service; measurable characteristics of service delivery."],["Quantization","Mapping values to a finite set of representable levels."],["Raster image","An image represented as a grid of pixels."],["RM","Rate Monotonic; fixed-priority scheduling based on task periods."],["Sampling","Measuring a continuous signal at discrete time intervals."],["Synchronization","Coordination of media timing and presentation relationships."],["Tweening","Generation of intermediate animation frames between keyframes."],["Vector graphics","Graphics represented using mathematical shapes, paths and attributes."]]
};